Slide 4 — Explicit design assumptions

    Use a hypothetical enterprise platform for the research model:
    - Clients include web applications, mobile applications, and partner integrations.
    - The backend contains independently deployed microservices.
    - Requests are primarily HTTP/HTTPS, with selected APIs potentially using streaming or other protocols.
    - Multiple gateway instances are deployed across failure domains.
    - The gateway has no authoritative ownership of business data.
    - The gateway must continue operating during an individual gateway-instance failure and should degrade safely when a dependency fails.

These are design assumptions, not measured facts. 
State them clearly so judges can distinguish the model from a production benchmark.


=====================================================================================================================================
“I begin by separating functional requirements from quality attributes. 
Functional requirements define what the gateway does;
non-functional requirements define how well and how safely it must do it.

An important architectural decision is that cross-cutting policies belong at the boundary,
while business invariants belong in domain services. 

If a payment must never be charged twice, for example, 
the gateway can propagate an idempotency key, 
but the payment service must enforce the actual idempotency guarantee.

I also make assumptions explicit. 
A research-quality architecture should not quietly assume unlimited network bandwidth,
perfect dependencies, or zero failure. 
These assumptions will drive the capacity model, 
fault analysis, and evaluation plan later in the presentation.”




