5. Slides 8–11 — the engineering decisions that differentiate the design
Slide 8 — routing and service discovery
    The gateway must determine where a request should go and how to reach a healthy instance.
    
    Consider a request:    
                        GET /api/v1/orders/123
    
    A routing policy may map /api/v1/orders/** to the Order Service. 
    Service discovery then supplies eligible endpoints, and load balancing selects an instance.

📘 ROUTING AND LOAD-BALANCING APPROACHES

🔹 APPROACH: 1. 🛣️ Static Routes
✅ Advantages: Simple and predictable
⚠️ Limitations: Changes require deployment or configuration updates
🎯 Appropriate Use: Small, stable systems

🔹 APPROACH: 2. 🔎 Dynamic Service Discovery
✅ Advantages: Adapts to instance changes
⚠️ Limitations: Discovery consistency and caching must be managed
🎯 Appropriate Use: Dynamic microservices

🔹 APPROACH: 3. ⚖️ Client-Side Load Balancing
✅ Advantages: Flexible, client-specific behavior
⚠️ Limitations: Clients carry more complexity
🎯 Appropriate Use: Controlled internal environments

🔹 APPROACH: 4. 🚪 Gateway-Side Load Balancing
✅ Advantages: Centralized policy and visibility
⚠️ Limitations: Gateway becomes responsible for upstream selection
🎯 Appropriate Use: Public API entry points

🔹 APPROACH: 5. 🕸️ Service Mesh
✅ Advantages: Rich east-west traffic controls
⚠️ Limitations: Additional operational and configuration complexity
🎯 Appropriate Use: Large service-to-service environments

Recommended approach: Use dynamic endpoint discovery when the service platform already supports it, 
with bounded caching, health-aware selection, and a defined fallback policy.

Do not assume that a successful health check guarantees a successful request. 
Health signals are imperfect and may lag behind failures.


