Slide 10 — rate limiting and overload protection
Rate limiting controls how much traffic a client or tenant may send. 
Overload protection controls whether the gateway or service has capacity to accept additional work.


📘 RATE-LIMITING AND TRAFFIC-CONTROL STRATEGIES

🔹 Strategy: Fixed Window
⚙️ Mechanism: Counts requests within a fixed time interval.
⚠️ Trade-off: Allows boundary bursts when requests accumulate
               at the end of one window and the start of the next.

🔹 Strategy: Sliding Window
⚙️ Mechanism: Tracks request traffic across a moving time interval.
⚠️ Trade-off: Higher state or computation cost.

🔹 Strategy: Token Bucket
⚙️ Mechanism: Tokens replenish at a defined rate. Requests consume
               available tokens, allowing controlled bursts.
⚠️ Trade-off: Permits bounded bursts, so instantaneous traffic can
               exceed the average configured rate.

🔹 Strategy: Leaky Bucket
⚙️ Mechanism: Smooths outgoing traffic toward a configured rate.
⚠️ Trade-off: May delay or reject bursts, depending on the
               implementation and queue capacity.

🔹 Strategy: Concurrency Limiting
⚙️ Mechanism: Caps the number of simultaneous in-flight requests.
⚠️ Trade-off: Requires correctly configured limits and awareness
               of workload duration and resource consumption.
These are related, but they are not identical.


Recommended design: Use local protection for fast overload rejection, 
combined with shared or partitioned quota enforcement when tenant-wide 
fairness requires it. Define behavior for limiter-store failure explicitly.

